{"kind":"data","event":{"type":"tool_call","status":"complete","toolCallId":"01a15348-6a2f-766d-9df4-34a87fe7ada1","toolName":"task","args":{},"result":"{\"lg_name\":\"Command\",\"update\":{\"files\":{},\"messages\":[{\"lc\":1,\"type\":\"constructor\",\"id\":[\"langchain_core\",\"messages\",\"ToolMessage\"],\"kwargs\":{\"content\":\"## Findings\\n1. [High] tier-price-bypass-via-strategic-withdrawals — buyers can lock in a lower price than the final clearing price\\n   Criterion: 2 adversary-resistant\\n   Location: `contracts/DemandPool.sol:DemandPool.commit` + `contracts/DemandPool.sol:DemandPool.withdraw` + `contracts/Settler.sol:Settler.settle` (approx lines 91, 113, 59)\\n   Why it matters: A buyer’s escrow is fixed at commit-time `newPrice`, but later withdrawals can increase final clearing price. In `settle`, `settledPrice = min(paid, cp)` means underfunded buyers are never charged up to final `cp`, so an attacker can use sybil commits/withdrawals to end with fewer buyers while still paying the earlier lower tier price, extracting value from the supplier and violating tiered-pricing integrity.\\n   Suggested fix: Make each committed buyer fully collateralize the maximum possible price (e.g., top-tier/initial tier price) and only refund down at settlement, or add a required top-up flow when price rises and block settlement for unpaid buyers. Settlement should not allow `paid < final clearing price` if final-tier pricing is intended to be binding.\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n- Assumed the requested “volume-tiered pricing” means final effective price should correspond to the final buyer count at settlement, not a historical commit-time snapshot per buyer.\\n\\n## Audit Provenance\\n| Subagent | Dispatched | Files audited | Corpus rules cited |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| functional-auditor | yes | 4 | — |\",\"tool_call_id\":\"toolu_bdrk_01N5XcWHi12ptAwhSYDTJbVv\",\"name\":\"task\",\"additional_kwargs\":{},\"response_metadata\":{}}}]},\"goto\":[]}"}}
